  "instruction": "Select ALL that apply — there are three correct answers. Then check your reasoning against the text.",
  "minCorrect": 3,
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "a",
      "text": "He refuses to postpone the executions, arguing that postponement would undermine the court's authority.",
      "correct": true,
      "explanation": "Danforth explicitly ties his decision to the court's credibility: 'Postponement now speaks a doubt on my capacity to judge.' He treats institutional reputation as evidence."
    },
    {
      "id": "b",
      "text": "He accepts spectral evidence — testimony about what the afflicted girls claim to see — as proof of guilt.",
      "correct": true,
      "explanation": "Spectral evidence is unfalsifiable: the accused cannot disprove a private vision. Danforth's reliance on it inverts the burden of proof."
    },
    {
      "id": "c",
      "text": "He forces Mary Warren to recant her testimony by confronting her with the girls' theatrical performance in the courtroom.",
      "correct": true,
      "explanation": "Mary's retraction is coerced through social pressure, not weighed as evidence. Danforth lets the spectacle substitute for cross-examination."
    },
    {
      "id": "d",
      "text": "He personally questions each accused villager in a private, unrecorded hearing before sentencing.",
      "correct": false,
      "explanation": "Nothing in the text shows Danforth conducting private hearings. This is an appealing but unsupported claim — the play's terror comes from the court's very public spectacle."
    },
    {
      "id": "e",
      "text": "He orders the arrests of Giles Corey and Francis Nurse so he can weigh their claims carefully before the executions proceed.",
      "correct": false,
      "explanation": "Giles dies under the press (peine forte et dure) rather than being given a hearing, and Francis is dismissed. The court suppresses their claims rather than weighing them."
    }
  ],
  "feedback": {
    "correctTitle": "All three — exactly right.",
    "correctBody": "These three details trace the same move: Danforth converts doubt about the court into treason against it. Note that each item replaces an evidence standard with an authority claim.",
    "incorrectTitle": "Not quite — review the highlighted rows.",
    "incorrectBody": "Focus on moments where Danforth substitutes power or spectacle for evidence. Two of the remaining options describe hearings or hearings-like care that never happens in the play."
  },
  "continueLabel": "Check answer",
  "retryLabel": "Try again",
  "tryAgainHint": "Look again at Act IV lines where Danforth explains why he cannot delay the executions — his reasoning is about the court, not the evidence.",
  "allowRetry": true
